• Clone and prepare a Node.js to-do app from Docker’s GitHub repository.
• Create a Dockerfile using node:12-alpine, install dependencies, and set the app to run on src/index.js.
• Build and run the Docker image, exposing port 3000 to access the app.
• Create a Docker network to enable communication between containers.
• Run a MySQL container on the network with environment variables for initialization.
• Connect the app container to the MySQL container using network alias and environment variables.
• Verify the app connects to MySQL and data persists across containers.

PRINCIPLES OF USING TWO DOCKET CONTAINERS:
• Containers ensure environment consistency, isolating dependencies for predictable deployment.
• Use separate volumes for data in production and bind mounts during development.
• Handle Linux signals properly in containers to manage process lifecycle and avoid zombies.
• Package one app per container to maintain lifecycle alignment and maximize container benefits.
• Keep images small, use common layers, and tag images effectively for efficient builds and deployments.
• Minimize attack surface by removing unnecessary tools and avoid running containers as root.

CONFIGURING AN APPLICATION RUNNING TWO DOCKERS:
• Log into Azure via Docker and create a Docker context linked to an Azure container instance.
• Use docker-compose to build and run a Python Flask app with Redis locally.
• Push the Docker image to Docker Hub for Azure Container Instances (ACI) deployment.
• Switch Docker context to Azure and deploy the app in the cloud using docker-compose up.
• Access the running app via the provided IP, confirming frontend and backend containers communicate successfully.

TWO DOCKERS IN TWO CLOUD ENVIRONMENTS:
• Multi-cloud uses multiple cloud providers for flexibility and best service selection.
• Benefits include agility, portability, faster application development, and innovation support.
• Container clustering enhances scalability, resilience, and flexible updates.
• Risks involve data volume limits, network performance, storage choices, security, and cost.
• Key infrastructure factors: container engine support, network and storage integration, orchestration tools like Kubernetes, and production readiness.

CONFIGURING TWO DOCKERS IN TWO CLOUD ENVIRONMENTS:
